      "problem": "Consider the equation $-2{x}^{2}-8x-8=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?",
      "worked_solution": "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-2,$ $b=-8,$ and $c=-8,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-8)^{2}-4(-2)(-8) \\\\  & =64-64 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution.",
